WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.745
BRIAN: Greg's coming
back now, and he's

00:00:01.745 --> 00:00:06.270
going to show you how we use GCP
and Firebase together to really

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.411
modernize corporate IT apps.

00:00:08.411 --> 00:00:08.910
Greg--

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.010
GREG: Thanks, Brian.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.250
Brian told you we're going to
take an example of an existing

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.750
on-prem application.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.090
And we're going to modernize
it with Google Cloud.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.160
Now, in this case, the
app that Chris and I

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.370
are going to work with is
an old ASP.NET application.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.147
Well, that's not an
ASP.NET application.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.230
That's an ASP.NET application.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.939
This is an application used
by an insurance company.

00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:48.480
So the adjusters go
out in the field.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.771
They take their digital camera.

00:00:49.771 --> 00:00:51.390
They take pictures
of the accidents.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.250
They put it in their SD cards.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.580
And then they upload the
images into the application.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.220
It's also got an API
on it so that you

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.770
can manage all the images that
are associated with a given

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:02.730
claim.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.340
Now we're going to start
bringing this application out

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.830
of the 90's and
into the modern era.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.810
We're going to start
by giving our agents

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.730
a good mobile
application so they don't

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.680
have to lug a laptop around.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.120
Firebase makes that super easy.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.880
You can build a
mobile application

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.012
without having to be
a back end expert.

00:01:21.012 --> 00:01:22.470
In this case, what
Chris is showing

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.190
you is the line of
code that integrates

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.170
Firebase with cloud storage.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.380
So this line of code allows
the camera to take a picture.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.090
That picture then
automatically gets

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.592
uploaded into a Google
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:01:35.592 --> 00:01:37.050
So to show this is
going to happen,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.240
Chris is going to take a
quick photo with the phone.

00:01:43.726 --> 00:01:45.350
CHRIS: All right,
everyone, say cheese.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.320
I love having audiences do that.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:50.820
GREG: OK.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.040
So that photo now
is being uploaded.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.710
And it's being stored into a
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.170
Now, to show that it
really is in a GCS bucket,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.545
we're going to
switch over, we're

00:01:59.545 --> 00:02:01.530
going to list the
contents of this bucket.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.180
Now, the Windows
users among you are

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.670
going to notice that
he's using PowerShell.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.000
That's because he's using
cloud tools for PowerShell.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.009
It gives windows developers
a first class experience

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.849
using Google Cloud Platform with
the services that they love.

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:15.390
And sure enough,
there's the picture.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.250
It was just uploaded today.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.850
So now we've got a
claim picture in GCS.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.820
How do we get it to this
old legacy application?

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.120
Apogee and cloud functions
make that simple.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.260
The good news is our legacy
app has an API, as I mentioned.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.790
The bad news is the app was
written over a decade ago

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.520
so it's a SOAP API.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.060
So it's not really very friendly
to modern RESTful developer

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.710
like Chris.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.710
Apogee, however, is an
enterprise API management

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:44.615
platform.

00:02:44.615 --> 00:02:45.990
And we're going
to start by using

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:50.010
it to create a proxy to
convert this legacy SOAP

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.260
XML into modern JSON.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.300
So here's the Apogee console.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.120
We've connected it to
the WSDL of the service.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.540
I can't believe I'm
saying that in 2017.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:00.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.330
And on the left side
you see the SOAP XML.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.760
And on the right side,
Apogee is automatically

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.520
created a much friendlier,
easier for the developer

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.460
to use, JSON version of it.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.450
Now Apogee has a lot
of other capabilities.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.540
So in addition to
doing that, Chris

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.200
has applied a quota, so that we
don't flood this poor service

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.822
with millions of mobile agents
all trying to upload photos

00:03:21.822 --> 00:03:22.530
at the same time.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.650
It automatically
provides throttling.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.420
We've also put
authentication in so

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.120
that only our agents and only
those mobile applications

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.510
can upload the data.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.310
Apogee has got tons
more capabilities.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.050
I am only scratching
the surface.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.450
But for our purposes that's good
enough for this application.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.450
So we got images in a bucket.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.890
We got our
application that's now

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.900
got a nice modern API on it.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.940
How do we connect the two?

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.020
Well, I could deploy a VM,
but that's overkill, right?

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.290
All I want to do is copy a
little file from GCS to an API.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:58.200
Why should I pick an operating
system and have to patch the OS

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.360
and deploy a big heavy VM image?

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.190
Cloud functions allows
me to just write

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.340
a snippet of JavaScript.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.080
This JavaScript is listening
to that GCS bucket.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.950
So anytime a file gets added,
this snippet gets invoked.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.900
And if it goes down,
you'll see he's

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.050
using the post method, that
nice RESTful interface,

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.850
onto our web service.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.599
So now when Chris
takes a picture,

00:04:19.599 --> 00:04:21.640
we should see the picture
go all the way through.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.102
Now, I don't have a car
crash on stage, Chris.

00:04:24.102 --> 00:04:25.560
I think you've got
though some sort

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.030
of a next best thing for us.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.220
CHRIS: Actually, what
I'm prepared to do,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.860
Greg, is, I've got some scale
model vehicle replicas here

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.640
that I'm going to use to
simulate a high speed collision

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.150
scenario.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.483
GREG: This is scaled down into--

00:04:40.483 --> 00:04:41.410
CHRIS: Exactly right.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:41.800
GREG: Very good.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:42.280
CHRIS: You're ready?

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:43.196
GREG: Yeah, go for it.

00:04:43.196 --> 00:04:47.800
CHRIS: [SOUNDS OF A CAR CRASH]

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.290
GREG: Please tell me you
didn't misplace the phone.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.670
So he's going to take the
picture of-- oh, the humanity.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.805
And it gets uploaded.

00:05:02.805 --> 00:05:04.180
And now he's going
to switch back

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.012
to our application,
our corporate app.

00:05:06.012 --> 00:05:07.720
He's going to hit F5
because it's the 90s

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.970
and we have to refresh.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:10.730
And there we go.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:11.730
There's our application.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.830
We started with the
legacy on-prem app.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.500
We used Firebase to build a
mobile application that didn't

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.330
even require a back end expert.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.740
We wrapped the old SOAP API and
modern JSON and cloud functions

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.070
with this wonderful glue layer
to connect everything together.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.530
But one more thing,
this application

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.079
started as an
on-prem application.

00:05:33.079 --> 00:05:35.370
And we've wrapped it with
all this amazing cloud stuff,

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.740
but it's still sitting on-prem.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.600
Let's fix that bug too.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.880
With just a couple
of clicks, Chris

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.080
will start the the Migration
Wizard and in about 45 minutes

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.330
we will live migrate
this running service

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.800
into Google Cloud
Platform, where

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.110
it runs on Google's amazing
infrastructure network

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.892
operations and reliability.

00:05:53.892 --> 00:05:55.350
And now we've really
taken this app

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.800
and brought it into
the modern era.

